lesson "Exponential & Logarithmic Derivatives" {
  course: "calculus"
  unit: "Derivatives & Rules"
  difficulty: "Intermediate"
  icon: "TrendingUp"
  summary: "The function that is its own derivative, the clean 1/x that falls out of ln(x), and every chain rule built on top of them."
  unit: "Derivatives & Rules"
  difficulty: "Intermediate"
  icon: "TrendingUp"
  summary: "The function that is its own derivative, the clean 1/x that falls out of ln(x), and every chain rule built on top of them."
  skills: ["deriv-exp", "deriv-log"]

  slide "A Function Equal to Its Own Derivative" {
    id: "d6-intro"
    cat: "The Exponential"
    > There's exactly one family of functions where the height *is* the slope:
    > $f(x) = e^{x}$. At every point, how far up the curve sits equals how
    > steep it's climbing there.
    >
    > $$\frac{d}{dx}\,e^{x} = e^{x}$$
  }

  slide "Height Equals Slope" {
    id: "d6-scene"
    cat: "The Exponential"
    > Drag the point along $e^{x}$ and watch the label: the tangent's slope
    > always matches the point's height, exactly.

    scene plane {
      x: [-2, 3]
      y: [-1, 15]
      grid
      axes
      param a = 1 { range: [-2, 3], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = exp(x) { color: primary, width: 3 }
      point P = (a, exp(a)) { drag: x -> a, color: accent, r: 7, label: "height = ${exp(a)}" }
      line tangent { through: P, slope: exp(a), color: success, width: 2 }
      label at (a + 0.2, exp(a) + 1.5) = "slope = ${exp(a)}" { color: success }
      slider a { label: "a" }
    }
  }

  slide "Differentiate the Exponential" {
    id: "d6-quiz-exp"
    cat: "The Exponential"
    quiz {
      ask "What is $\dfrac{d}{dx}\,e^{x}$?"
      skill: "deriv-exp"
      - "$x\,e^{x-1}$" { why: "That's the power rule pattern, which doesn't apply here: the variable is in the exponent, not the base." }
      * "$e^{x}$"
      - "$0$" { why: "e^x is never constant, it keeps growing." }
      - "$e$" { why: "The derivative is a whole function of x, not a single number." }
      hint "This is the one function that's its own derivative."
      ! "e^x differentiates to itself: that's the defining property of e."
    }
  }

  slide "Chain Rule Meets the Exponential" {
    id: "d6-chain-exp"
    cat: "The Exponential"
    > Stack a function inside the exponent and the chain rule kicks in as
    > usual: keep the exponential, then multiply by the derivative of what's
    > inside.
    >
    > $$\frac{d}{dx}\,e^{g(x)} = e^{g(x)}\cdot g'(x)$$
    >
    > So $\frac{d}{dx}\,e^{3x} = 3e^{3x}$: the exponential stays, and the inside's
    > derivative (3) tags along as a constant multiplier.
  }

  slide "Evaluate the Chain Rule" {
    id: "d6-numeric-chain-exp"
    cat: "The Exponential"
    numeric {
      ask "Let $h(x) = e^{2x}$. Find $h'(0)$."
      skill: "deriv-exp"
      answer: 2
      hint "h'(x) = e^{2x} · 2, the exponential times the derivative of 2x."
      ! "h'(x) = 2e^{2x}, so h'(0) = 2e^0 = 2 · 1 = 2."
    }
  }

  slide "The Natural Log's Slope" {
    id: "d6-log-scene"
    cat: "The Logarithm"
    > $\ln(x)$, the inverse of $e^{x}$, has its own clean derivative:
    >
    > $$\frac{d}{dx}\,\ln(x) = \frac{1}{x}$$
    >
    > Drag the point and watch the slope shrink as $x$ grows, always matching
    > $\frac{1}{x}$.

    scene plane {
      x: [0.1, 6]
      y: [-3, 3]
      grid
      axes
      param a = 1 { range: [0.2, 5.8], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = log(x) { color: primary, width: 3 }
      point P = (a, log(a)) { drag: x -> a, color: accent, r: 7, label: "ln(a) = ${log(a)}" }
      line tangent { through: P, slope: 1 / a, color: success, width: 2 }
      label at (a + 0.3, log(a) + 1) = "slope = ${1 / a}" { color: success }
      slider a { label: "a" }
    }
  }

  slide "Differentiate the Logarithm" {
    id: "d6-quiz-log"
    cat: "The Logarithm"
    quiz {
      ask "What is $\dfrac{d}{dx}\,\ln(x)$?"
      skill: "deriv-log"
      - "$\ln(x)$" { why: "Unlike e^x, ln(x) isn't its own derivative." }
      * "$\dfrac{1}{x}$"
      - "$x$" { why: "That's not what falls out of differentiating a logarithm." }
      - "$\dfrac{1}{x^{2}}$" { why: "Close in shape, but the derivative of ln(x) only has x to the first power in the denominator." }
      hint "This is one of the cleanest derivatives in calculus: a whole logarithm collapses to a simple reciprocal."
      ! "d/dx[ln(x)] = 1/x, one of the tidiest results in calculus."
    }
  }

  slide "Chain Rule Meets the Logarithm" {
    id: "d6-chain-log"
    cat: "The Logarithm"
    > Same idea as before: keep the reciprocal structure, then multiply by the
    > derivative of what's inside.
    >
    > $$\frac{d}{dx}\,\ln\!\left(g(x)\right) = \frac{g'(x)}{g(x)}$$
    >
    > So $\frac{d}{dx}\,\ln\!\left(x^{2}+1\right) = \dfrac{2x}{x^{2}+1}$.
  }

  slide "Evaluate the Log Chain Rule" {
    id: "d6-numeric-chain-log"
    cat: "The Logarithm"
    numeric {
      ask "Let $h(x) = \ln\!\left(x^{2}+1\right)$. Find $h'(1)$."
      skill: "deriv-log"
      answer: 1
      hint "h'(x) = (2x)/(x²+1), the derivative of the inside over the inside."
      ! "h'(x) = 2x/(x²+1), so h'(1) = 2/2 = 1."
    }
  }

  slide "Other Bases" {
    id: "d6-quiz-base"
    cat: "Other Bases"
    quiz {
      ask "What is $\dfrac{d}{dx}\,2^{x}$?"
      skill: "deriv-exp"
      - "$2^{x}$" { why: "That's only true for base e. Every other base carries an extra constant factor." }
      * "$2^{x}\ln(2)$"
      - "$x\cdot 2^{x-1}$" { why: "That's the power-rule pattern for x raised to a fixed power, not a fixed base raised to x." }
      - "$\ln(2)$" { why: "The derivative is a whole function of x, not a single number." }
      hint "Only base e avoids picking up an extra constant. Every other base tags on ln(base)."
      ! "d/dx[a^x] = a^x·ln(a), so d/dx[2^x] = 2^x·ln(2)."
    }
  }

  slide "Match Each Rule" {
    id: "d6-match"
    cat: "Other Bases"
    match {
      ask "Match each derivative rule to its result."
      skill: "deriv-exp"
      pair "d/dx[e^x]" -> "e^x"
      pair "d/dx[ln(x)]" -> "1/x"
      pair "d/dx[e^{g(x)}]" -> "g'(x)·e^{g(x)}"
      pair "d/dx[ln(g(x))]" -> "g'(x)/g(x)"
      decoy "x·e^{x-1}"
      ! "Every exponential/log rule follows the same shape: keep the outer function, multiply by the inside's derivative."
    }
  }

  slide "Put It All Together" {
    id: "d6-capstone"
    cat: "Other Bases"
    numeric {
      ask "Let $f(x) = x\,e^{x}$. Find $f'(0)$."
      skill: "deriv-exp"
      answer: 1
      hint "This needs the product rule: f'(x) = (1)e^x + x·e^x."
      ! "By the product rule, f'(x) = e^x + x·e^x = e^x(1+x). At x = 0, that's 1·(1+0) = 1."
    }
  }
}
